const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens common in Norway?', a: 'Yes. The raven (Corvus corax) is a common resident throughout Norway, including above the Arctic Circle. Norway\'s large areas of wilderness — mountain plateaus (fjell), boreal forest, coastline, and fjords — provide extensive raven habitat. Estimates for the Norwegian breeding population are in the range of 15,000–20,000 pairs, making it one of the highest raven densities in Europe.' },
  { q: 'What is the Norwegian word for raven?', a: 'The Norwegian word for raven is "ravn" (Bokmål/Nynorsk). This derives directly from the Old Norse hrafn — the same word found in Icelandic hrafn, Swedish korp (a related Germanic form), and historically in English as hrœfn or hrœfn. The Old Norse hrafn is everywhere in Norwegian place names: Ravnefjell (Raven Mountain), Ravnøya (Raven Island), and many others across the country.' },
  { q: 'What role does the raven play in Norse mythology from Norway?', a: 'Norway was the heartland of Viking Age Norse culture and the raven\'s mythological role is deeply embedded here. Odin\'s ravens Huginn and Muninn — Thought and Memory — were believed to fly the world daily and return to whisper what they had seen into Odin\'s ears. The raven banner (Hrafnsmerki) was used by Norwegian Viking chieftains and kings. Harald Fairhair\'s era (late 9th century) saw raven symbolism prominently used in Norwegian warrior culture.' },
];
